so we've been covering Donald Trump's  election of Judge Brett Kavanaugh to  replace Supreme Court Justice Anthony  Kennedy and we talked yesterday about  how immediately upon his selection there  started to be sort of a propaganda war  fought on Wikipedia around what his  position is on Roe vs. Wade and on  abortion rights and there has been a  sort of counter reaction from some  including people emailing me saying you  know David it's not going to be as bad  as we think it is when it comes to roe v  wade it's not likely roe v wade will  actually be repealed just because Brett  Kavanaugh is on the court and then  others saying you know it doesn't really  matter whether roe v wade is repealed  there's other ways that Brett  Cavanaugh's presence on the court will  de facto attack abortion rights and I  think that this sort of goes to the most  important analysis that we can do about  Brett Kavanaugh which is not so much  figuring out exactly what he meant when  he had his federal judgeship  confirmation hearings or even looking at  what are the ways in which Wikipedia the  Wikipedia article about him has been  modified 20 times when it comes to his  position on abortion the question is  let's think about how replacing Anthony  Kennedy with Brett Kavanaugh in general  is going to change the breakup of the  court right let's see if we can make an  assessment about that because the  balance and the way in which the court  will more generally approach issues of  corporate power religious liberties  individual rights and so on that's  really what's important more so than  exactly how Brett Cavanaugh would feel  if case a B C or D came before him on  the court so question one is how does  replacing Kennedy with Kavanagh change  the balance of the court  if you imagine a sort of political  spectrum from left to right progressive  to conservative on which we place the  nine Supreme Court justices let's  actually look at what the balance would  be if we were to replace Kennedy with  Cavanaugh and the New York Times  actually had a graphic that is exactly  this which they created when Neil  Gorsuch was selected on the assumption  that Gore such would be appointed which  he ultimately was we've got a spectrum  from left to right where you've got Ruth  Bader Ginsburg at the  furthest left of the court and Clarence  Thomas at the furthest right of the  court and you see Anthony Kennedy more  to the left than Center but importantly  he's guy number five in other words  there's four people to the left of  Anthony Kennedy and there's four people  to the right of Anthony Kennedy and what  is it that doesn't know and this graph  actually still includes Antonin Scalia  who is of course not not on the court  anymore so of the current justices for  the look to the left four to the right  the assessments of Brett Kavanaugh are  that he would be to the right of most of  the court but slightly to the left of  Clarence Thomas so I've actually drawn  in a vertical arrow sort of placing  Brett Kavanaugh on this political  spectrum to the right of Neil Gorsuch  but to the left of Clarence Thomas the  average of the court is going to move to  the right right the mean of where the  court is politically is going to move to  the right because you are moving from  Anthony Kennedy to the vertical arrow on  this graph which is where Brett  Kavanaugh would be what does this mean  this means that John Roberts who I have  put in a blue square on this image John  Roberts becomes the justice who is in  the middle so to speak with four  justices to the left and four justices  to the right that is a real change okay  when the swing vote when the most  centrist vote is John Roberts rather  than Anthony Kennedy not only does the  court move to the right but you can  start to think about how the divided  cases would go how would the 5-4  decisions go when you look at how  Roberts has decided on lots of 5-4  decisions in the past and you realize  that replacing Kennedy with Cavanaugh  means that this becomes a more  pro-corporate Supreme Court that this  becomes a more so-called Pro religious  liberties Supreme Court so the abortion  topic it's an important topic and we'll  have to see how quickly abortion rights  are attacked but what we know is that  with Roberts becoming the swing and  Cavanaugh being  slightly to the left of Clarence Thomas  this becomes a decidedly even more  pro-corporate  court and the Supreme Court is known for  that the supreme court generally decides  big-picture all else being equal for the  party with more power or for the  corporation however you want to sort of  divvy it up that's going to become even  worse with this move and that's much  more important than will roe v-- wade be  repealed yeah and that's why in some  respects this is even worse than Neal  Gorsuch going on to the Supreme Court  because with Gorsuch you were just  replacing a far right winger in Antonin  Scalia with a far right winger and gore  said correct now you're replacing a  centrist Anthony Kennedy with someone  who's on the far right the only thing  worse than that would be if you know  Ruth Bader Ginsburg Estonia MS Sotomayor  retired for some reason and yes was  replaced with the right winger but it's  really bad this is the only worse  scenario would be if someone like Ruth  Bader Ginsburg or Sotomayor were to  leave the court while Donald Trump is  president now relevant to this  particular president and who knows how  many years this will be important for it  could be many there's the question of  Bret Cavanaugh's views on the  investigation of presidents and whether  this played a role or not in Donald  Trump's election we don't know  particularly because it turns out that  Anthony Kennedy was actually involved in  the selection of Bret Cavanaugh which  we'll talk about a little bit next  Cavanaugh was part of an investigation  of Bill Clinton in the 1990s he worked  for ken starr's independent  investigation of Bill Clinton but now  Brett Kavanaugh believes that not only  can you not indict a sitting president  that you shouldn't even really burden a  sitting president with an investigation  and when you ask isn't that a double  standard because he was part of an  investigation of a president and now he  says you shouldn't investigate  presidents Cavanaugh says he regrets his  role in the Ken Starr investigation of  Bill Clinton that could prove hugely  relevant to Donald Trump if there are  issues related to Trump's possible  crimes visa vie the Robert Moeller  investigation if those issues get to the  Supreme Court the fact that Brett  Kavanaugh doesn't even think presidents  should be burdened by investigations  could very quickly become  now let's deal with the roe v wade and  the abortion issue I've had another 24  hours to research this and think about  it I'm more convinced than I was  yesterday that it's possible that roe v  wade might go under this court Kavanaugh  would be on the court for a very long  time but I don't think it's gonna be the  immediate threat to abortion it's too  much of a hot-button issue to straight  up  reverse roe v wade and the more likely  scenario is that abortion will suffer a  sort of death by a thousand cuts with  little restrictions coming out of the  states upheld and approved by the  Supreme Court and as Jeffrey Toobin said  we could defacto get to a dozen and a  half states with abortion bans or  effective abortion bans practically  speaking abortion bans within a couple  of years of this confirmation if it  indeed happens and I think that you know  case in point there's a handful of  states that already make it incredibly  difficult to get a safe and legal  abortion and we have roe v wade so you  can expand abortion rights or restrict  abortion rights without actually  touching roe v wade merely with the  supreme court making certain decisions  when it comes to state attempts to  curtail abortion rights and more than  likely John Roberts the Chief Justice  and the guy who will be the newfound  swing vote he and other conservatives  understand that if you overturn roe v  wade it attracts a ton of attention it  doesn't immediately change anything  because you still actually need  individual states to make decisions  about what they want the law to be when  it comes to abortion if you quietly  continually uphold small restrictions in  Texas this other restriction in Arkansas  whatever that can restrict abortion  significantly without the headline of  roe v wade overturned gay marriage is  sort of under the same lines gay  marriage has been mentioned as well as  are we going to see a reversal of the  Supreme Court's decision that states  can't ban same-sex marriage I don't  think you're gonna see that anytime soon  because Chief Justice Roberts we know is  concerned with sort of his legacy as  chief justice of the Supreme Court  he knows it wouldn't look good for a  court to make one decision in 2015 and  then in 2018 or 2019 very soon  thereafter make a decision that is the  exact opposite of that he knows that but  the new Avenue is religious freedom to  justify or defend discrimination against  LGBT individuals so I think if you're  going to see a tax on LGBT rights and  you may well see them it's not going to  be just overturning the ban on States  banning same-sex marriage it's going to  be this new wave of anti LGBT activism  which is so-called religious freedom  whatever you want to make as a micro  analysis the macro analysis is that this  is a huge conservative victory whether  Trump is a conservative or just played  one on TV to win the GOP primary doesn't  matter because this is going to be the  biggest real sustaining influence of  Trump by Trump on the United States and  then it comes to can we do anything to  stop it  and I got a few emails from people  saying you know it's a risk for  Democrats to obstruct if they're even  able to because they might suffer during  the midterms I don't know about that and  anecdotally we put a poll up on the  community tab on the David Pakman show  YouTube channel asking should Senate  Democrats obstruct the Supreme Court  pick now the reaction is of course from  our mostly left-wing audience and the  mostly left-wing audience overwhelmingly  thinks that yes  on the basis of how Republicans treated  Merrick garland or at least on the basis  of waiting until voters have had their  say at the midterms 89% of our audience  thinks that Senate Democrats should  obstruct the SCOTUS pick from Donald  Trump so I get that it's just our  audience but the bigger picture Pat is  that it doesn't seem Democrats will be  punished by voters if they get in the  way of this if they can and we don't  know whether they'll be able to yeah I  think Democrats should be able to do  whatever they can right and they should  you have that option at their hands but  they don't really have the votes they  don't that's really the problem here and  you know Republicans got away with  stealing one Supreme Court pick they'll  get away with this one as well more than  it's out of our hands it's not like the  court has to listen to public opinion  these  we'll be on the court for thirty years  or so we do the caveat would be if you  get a couple Republicans to go the other  way but at the same time if someone like  Joe Manchin is going to just support  Cavanaugh anyway you sort of zero that  out so we'll continue to talk about that

